  "lines-and-slope.line1": "Line 1",
  "lines-and-slope.line2": "Line 2",
  "lines-and-slope.wh.same": "The slopes are equal ({a} and {b}), and so are the intercepts: these are two ways of writing the <strong>same line</strong> — every point on one is on the other. (Some books count that as parallel; it’s really one line.)",
  "lines-and-slope.wh.bothVert": "Both lines are vertical, side by side: <strong>parallel</strong>.",
  "lines-and-slope.wh.par": "The slopes are equal — <b>{a}</b> and <b>{b}</b> — but the lines are in different places (different <i class=\"vr\">y</i>-intercepts), so they never meet: <strong>parallel</strong>.",
  "lines-and-slope.wh.vertHoriz": "One line is vertical and the other horizontal, so they cross at a right angle: <strong>perpendicular</strong>.",
  "lines-and-slope.wh.perp": "Multiply the slopes: <b>{a} · {b} = −1</b>. Slopes that multiply to −1 are negative reciprocals (flip one and change its sign to get the other), so the lines cross at a right angle: <strong>perpendicular</strong>.",
  "lines-and-slope.wh.neitherProd": "The slopes, <b>{a}</b> and <b>{b}</b>, aren’t equal, and they multiply to <b>{p}</b>, not −1. So the lines cross, but not at a right angle: <strong>neither</strong>.",
  "lines-and-slope.wh.neither": "The slopes, <b>{a}</b> and <b>{b}</b>, aren’t equal. So the lines cross, but not at a right angle: <strong>neither</strong>.",
