  // ---- checking an answer ----
  "lines-and-slope.ck.flatNotUndef": "The rise is 0, and 0 divided by a number is just 0 — a flat line has slope <b>0</b>. “Undefined” is for a vertical line, where the <i>run</i> is 0.",
  "lines-and-slope.ck.notVert": "This line isn’t vertical — the run isn’t 0 — so its slope is a number. Work out rise ÷ run.",
  "lines-and-slope.ck.unreadSlope": "I can’t read <b>{s}</b> as a slope. Type a number like <b>2</b>, <b>−3/4</b> or <b>0.5</b> — or <b>undefined</b> for a vertical line.",
  "lines-and-slope.ck.zeroNotUndef": "Careful — 0 and undefined are different. A vertical line has a <i>run</i> of 0, and you can’t divide by 0, so its slope is <b>undefined</b>, not 0. (Slope 0 is a flat line.)",
  "lines-and-slope.ck.lookRun": "Look at the run: the <i class=\"vr\">x</i>-values don’t change, so the run is 0 — and you can’t divide by 0.",
  "lines-and-slope.ck.simplifyIt": "Right value — now simplify it: <b>{e}</b>.",
  "lines-and-slope.ck.rounded": "Rounded is fine — exactly, it’s <b>{v}</b>.",
  "lines-and-slope.ck.runOverRise": "That’s run over rise — upside down. Slope is <b>rise</b> (the change in <i class=\"vr\">y</i>) over <b>run</b> (the change in <i class=\"vr\">x</i>).",
  "lines-and-slope.ck.upsideSign": "That’s upside down, and the sign is off too. Slope is rise (change in <i class=\"vr\">y</i>) over run (change in <i class=\"vr\">x</i>).",
  "lines-and-slope.ck.signTwo": "The size is right, but check the sign. Subtract in the same order on the top and the bottom: <i class=\"vr\">y<sub>2</sub></i> − <i class=\"vr\">y<sub>1</sub></i> over <i class=\"vr\">x<sub>2</sub></i> − <i class=\"vr\">x<sub>1</sub></i> — both start with the second point.",
  "lines-and-slope.ck.signStd": "Check the sign. Moving the <i class=\"vr\">x</i>-term across the = changes its sign — for A<i class=\"vr\">x</i> + B<i class=\"vr\">y</i> = C the slope is <b>−A/B</b>.",
  "lines-and-slope.ck.sign": "The size is right, but check the sign: does the line go up or down from left to right?",
  "lines-and-slope.ck.upside": "That’s upside down. Slope is rise over run — the change in <i class=\"vr\">y</i> goes on top.",
  "lines-and-slope.ck.negRecip": "That’s the <i>negative reciprocal</i> — the slope of a line perpendicular to this one.",
  "lines-and-slope.ck.differences": "Slope uses <i>differences</i> — subtract the coordinates, don’t add them.",
  "lines-and-slope.ck.onePoint": "Slope compares the two points: the <i>change</i> in <i class=\"vr\">y</i> over the <i>change</i> in <i class=\"vr\">x</i> — not one point’s <i class=\"vr\">y</i> ÷ <i class=\"vr\">x</i>.",
  "lines-and-slope.ck.thatsB": "That’s <b>b</b>, the <i class=\"vr\">y</i>-intercept. The slope is the number multiplying <i class=\"vr\">x</i> once <i class=\"vr\">y</i> is by itself.",
  "lines-and-slope.ck.thatsA": "That’s A, the number in front of <i class=\"vr\">x</i> — but <i class=\"vr\">y</i> isn’t by itself yet. Get <i class=\"vr\">y</i> alone first (or use m = −A/B).",
  "lines-and-slope.ck.coord": "That’s a coordinate of the point. In point-slope form the slope is the number in front of the parentheses.",
  "lines-and-slope.ns.frac": "the fraction <b>{f}</b> is <b>{v}</b>.",
  "lines-and-slope.ns.combine": "multiply out and combine like terms, to get <b>{e}</b>.",
  "lines-and-slope.ns.divide": "every number can be divided by <b>{g}</b>.",
  "lines-and-slope.ck.notEq": "I can’t read that as an equation.",
  "lines-and-slope.ck.typeLike": " Type it like <b>y = 2x + 1</b>.",
  "lines-and-slope.ck.rightSimp": "Right line — now simplify it: {s}",
  "lines-and-slope.ck.inSI": "In slope-intercept form that’s <b>{e}</b>.",
  "lines-and-slope.ck.toSI": "Right line — now simplify it into slope-intercept form: get <i class=\"vr\">y</i> by itself, <b><i class=\"vr\">y</i> = <i class=\"vr\">m</i><i class=\"vr\">x</i> + <i class=\"vr\">b</i></b>.",
  "lines-and-slope.ck.xFirst": "(It’s usually written with the <i class=\"vr\">x</i>-term first: <b>{e}</b>.)",
  "lines-and-slope.ck.toStd": "Right line — now simplify it into standard form: the <i class=\"vr\">x</i>- and <i class=\"vr\">y</i>-terms on the left, the plain number on the right (A<i class=\"vr\">x</i> + B<i class=\"vr\">y</i> = C).",
  "lines-and-slope.ck.stdFrac": "Right line — now simplify it: standard form uses whole numbers. Multiply every term by <b>{d}</b> to clear the fractions.",
  "lines-and-slope.ck.stdGcd": "Right value — now simplify it: every number can be divided by <b>{g}</b>.",
  "lines-and-slope.ck.stdNeg": "Right line — now simplify the signs: standard form starts with a positive <i class=\"vr\">x</i>-term, so multiply every term by −1.",
  "lines-and-slope.ck.toPS": "Right line — but not simplified into point-slope form yet. Write it as <b><i class=\"vr\">y</i> − <i class=\"vr\">y<sub>1</sub></i> = <i class=\"vr\">m</i>(<i class=\"vr\">x</i> − <i class=\"vr\">x<sub>1</sub></i>)</b>, using the slope and one point on the line.",
